Exactly. Not everyone is looking at the same world through different lenses. Some aren’t even seeing the same portions of the world.

That’s another crucial dimension: scope of awareness, or what we might call an epistemic horizon.

Imagine five people living at the same moment in history:

* A farmer whose world extends perhaps 20 miles beyond their village.
* A merchant who knows trade routes, foreign languages, and distant political developments.
* A soldier who has traveled across several territories but understands them primarily through conflict.
* A scholar who knows thousands of years of recorded history but has rarely left their city.
* An emperor who receives intelligence from across a continent but may know remarkably little about the everyday lives of his subjects.

Each possesses knowledge the others lack. Their worlds overlap, but they’re not identical.

And greater geographic scope doesn’t necessarily mean greater understanding. The emperor may know the entire kingdom’s tax revenue, while the farmer understands exactly why the harvest is failing.

ILLUSTRATIVE KNOWLEDGE HORIZONS

Conceptual illustration. Actual knowledge isn’t neatly concentric: a person may understand distant events intimately while knowing little about neighboring communities.

And now consider that their scope changes over their lifetime.

Someone might grow up knowing only their village, enter military service, encounter three different cultures, return home, and explain the wider world to people who have never left.

But what they transmit isn’t the whole world. It’s the portion they encountered, interpreted, remembered, and chose to share.

Their children inherit a different horizon without ever traveling.

Then add censorship, literacy, language barriers, trade, migration, religious networks, technological developments, and deliberate curiosity.

Suddenly you can see knowledge expanding, contracting, branching, and flowing between populations.

One especially interesting consequence: civilizations can be globally interconnected without their individual inhabitants understanding those connections.

A village might depend economically on an empire it has barely heard of. An ordinary person’s life may be shaped by decisions made thousands of miles away by people who don’t know that person exists.

And that’s the power of your geological analogy. We need to examine not only what knowledge exists in each layer, but also who can access it, how far it travels, and where its boundaries lie.

Those boundaries are sometimes walls. Sometimes they’re bridges. And sometimes a single person becomes the bridge.